#if(v_ch11_saved_rose == 1) {
 

"Barry!" Rose's voice cuts in through bursts of static on the transceiver. "--happened-- everyone alright?--"

"Rose, I'm barely catching every third word," I say into the casing. "Too much seredium in the rock. We're in one piece, but we don't have our bearings yet."

"I didn't-- catch all that," Rose says. "Where did you-- teleported?"

"Still working on that," I say. "Some network of caves we haven't seen before. We need to scout a way through. I'll open the channel again once we reach open air."

"Understood, Barry," she says, her voice fragmenting into hiss. "-- careful!"

"You don't think this connects to the rebel passages?" Kate asks as I close the link and slide the transceiver into my tunic.

"No. I walked those tunnels all afternoon. The stone cut is wrong, and the layout doesn't match anything on their maps. We're still somewhere beneath Ollendor, though."

"Barry is right," Breynan says. "I know every turn of our network, including the sealed perimeter. This isn't rebel ground. There could be patrols nearby. Keep your weapons ready."

"I don't pick up any signatures nearby, though reading through this much seredium is like looking through muddy water," Melindra says, her fingers trailing the rough stone. "I can feel the faint trace of foot traffic on the streets overhead, so the ceiling layer must be fairly thin, but some of the side bulkheads are thick enough to swallow magical sense entirely. What I can tell you is that these corridors are laced with warding traps. We're not getting out of here without untangling at least a few."

"Traps," I mutter. "Daren would have had those cracked in five minutes. Does anyone here know the first thing about field dispelling? Illuna, Flower said you two have cleared dozens of ruins. What did you do with the wards?"

"Ancient ruins run on mechanical counterweights and pressure plates," Illuna says, her expression flat. "Flower flips over tripwires and drops past swinging scythes. The few times we ran into active enchantment, Arraka tore it down from the amulet. Flower and I are elementalists. Complex ward dispelling is not our discipline."

"Arraka can cast from inside that gold cage?" Kate asks. "I thought the binding choked off her casting."

"Dispelling is structure, not raw mass," Illuna says. "Her limitation in the vessel is capacity. She holds almost no reserve within the gold at any one second, so the only way she can cast anything destructive is to draw ambient energy through the metal seam for hours on end without a single lapse in focus. The knowledge never left her. That is why she can project solid-light targets, detailed illusions, and intricate dispelling arrays, yet cannot simply blast the amulet apart. A localized explosion requires three uninterrupted hours of gathering power. Disarming an elemental trap takes her thirty minutes."

}
#if(v_ch11_saved_rose == 0) {
 

"What's wrong?" Kate's voice is taut as she hovers over Breynan, watching him kneel beside Illuna with hands glowing in faint, unsteady light. "Why isn't she waking up?"

"I'm a soldier, not a white mage," Breynan mutters, wiping sweat from his temple. "I can stitch a gash or close a shallow puncture, but a chest wound like this is far beyond my training. Let me keep my focus. Stabilizing her is taking everything I have."

Kate presses her lips together. "Right. Do what you can."

We wait in the damp silence of the cavern while his light slowly knits the torn flesh. When he finally pulls his hands away and stands, he looks worn out.

"The bleeding is stopped," Breynan says. "The fatal damage is sealed, but she lost an immense amount of blood. She won't be in fighting shape for a long time."

I gesture toward the damp, jagged rock around us. "Do you recognize these caverns? I covered most of the rebel sectors earlier, and this doesn't match."

"It is not our territory," Breynan says. "We are still somewhere under the city, but whether these tunnels are abandoned or crawling with royal scouts, I cannot say. Stay on guard."

"I don't pick up any signatures nearby, though reading through this much seredium is like looking through muddy water," Melindra says, her fingers trailing the rough stone. "I can feel the faint trace of foot traffic on the streets overhead, so the ceiling layer must be fairly thin, but some of the side bulkheads are thick enough to swallow magical sense entirely. What I can tell you is that these corridors are laced with warding traps. We'll have to disarm them if we want to reach the surface."

"Traps," I mutter. "Daren would have had those cracked in five minutes. Does anyone else here know how to dismantle an active ward?"

"I know basic counter-enchantment," Breynan says. "Though I'll need to examine the nodes before I can tell you if my knowledge is enough."

"We cannot move until Illuna regains consciousness," Kate says. "Lifting her over rough terrain will tear Breynan's stitches open."

"I am... already conscious," Illuna's voice rasps from the floor.

She pushes herself up on trembling elbows. Her skin is drained of color, her bright blue eyes struggling to hold focus. Every breath costs her.

"Illuna, stay down," Kate says, stepping forward. "You need to rest!"

}
choice("Continue", B3-Ch04a-Intro2, v_current_scene = B3-Ch04a-Intro2, v_checkpoint_rich = 0)


ID: B3-Ch04a-Intro2
TEXT:

#if(v_ch11_saved_rose == 1) {
 "Arraka is half a city away with Daren, so you're our best option," I tell Illuna. "You've watched her dismantle wards for twenty years. You know the mechanics."

"Watching a master unravel a spell and executing the inversion yourself are not the same thing," Illuna says.

"I have some experience with basic warding," Breynan says. "Perhaps between the two of us we can work through it. Either way, we cannot linger. I tried reaching Barry in the teleporter chamber, but the signal dies immediately. Too many dense seredium bulkheads between here and the base at this depth. We'll have to climb toward the surface before he can lock onto our coordinates again."

Without a word of warning, Illuna gathers an orb of cold water in the air above Leila and drops it straight onto her face.

Leila jerks upright, sputtering, hands flying to her belt daggers as her eyes dart across the damp cavern.

"What happened?" her bright blue script flares in the air. "Where are the royal recruits?"

"Still above ground," Illuna says smoothly. "A faulty jump dropped us into an uncharted cavern system. We are finding our way to the surface. We can talk while we walk."

}
#if(v_ch11_saved_rose == 0) {
 "There is no time to rest," Illuna whispers. She gets her boots under her, sways dangerously, and catches herself against the cavern wall. "We could be directly beneath an enemy garrison. We move."

She pushes off the rock and stumbles after two paces. Kate and I catch her by both arms before she hits the gravel.

"I have it," Illuna mutters, pulling her arms back with cold precision. "I can walk."

"The banshee is right about our time," Breynan says. "We cannot afford to stay in the open. I tried reaching Barry in the teleporter chamber, but the signal dies immediately. Too many dense seredium bulkheads between here and the base at this depth. We'll have to climb toward the surface before he can lock onto our coordinates again."

Without a word of warning, Illuna gathers an orb of cold water in the air above Leila and drops it straight onto her face.

Leila jerks upright, sputtering, hands flying to her belt daggers as her eyes dart across the damp cavern.

"What happened?" her bright blue script flares in the air. "Where are the royal recruits?"

"Still above ground," Illuna says smoothly. "A faulty jump dropped us into an uncharted cavern system. We are finding our way to the surface. We can talk while we walk."

}
 Breynan channels a brief pulse of white light into Leila's temples to clear the lingering effects of her head impact against the cobbles, and we set off toward the highest incline. Melindra walks point, steering us around the strongest magical signatures embedded in the stone floor, though she warns us that the tunnel network narrows ahead and we will soon run out of bypass routes.

As we navigate the dark passage, a sharp chime rings out from my coat pocket.

I pull out the stat booster. The screen glows pale green in the cavern gloom, displaying a notification about the experimental aura parameters, followed by a prompt asking if I want a summary of the new functions.

The standard interface is locked until I respond to the prompt.

choice("See the introduction", B3-Ch04a-Introduction, v_current_scene = B3-Ch04a-Introduction, v_b3_ch4_introduction = 1)
