ID: B3-Ch04a-Avg-Joe
TEXT:

 "Did the technician manage to unlock them?" Kate asks as I slide the booster back into my pocket.

"He did," I say. "Hardware is online and all three auxiliary parameters are operational."

"And are you actually going to allocate points to test them this time, or are you leaving everything unspent like before?" Her brow furrows.

choice(""I'm not spending any points. I'll only start investing them when it's absolutely necessary."", B3-Ch04a-Avg-Joe2, v_current_scene = B3-Ch04a-Avg-Joe2, v_b3_ch4_demolisher = 1)
choice(""Listen, the stat device was always just a backup plan. I don't want to start relying on it."", B3-Ch04a-Avg-Joe2, v_current_scene = B3-Ch04a-Avg-Joe2, v_b3_ch4_demolisher = 2)
choice(""Not on this save slot. I'm using this one for the Average Joe achievement."", B3-Ch04a-Avg-Joe2, v_current_scene = B3-Ch04a-Avg-Joe2, v_b3_ch4_demolisher = 3)


ID: B3-Ch04a-Avg-Joe2
TEXT:

set(v_ac_b3_ch4_demolisher,1) if (v_b3_ch4_demolisher == 3)
achievement("Fourth wall demolisher",v_ac_b3_ch4_demolisher)
#if(v_b3_ch4_demolisher == 1 && v_b3_ch1_unlock != 2) {
 "I'm not spending any points. I'll only start investing them when it's absolutely necessary."

"I don't know why I expected a different answer," Kate says, shaking her head.

"Barry, aren't you even a little bit curious to see what they do?" Leila's bright blue letters form in the air.

"A little," I say. "Mainly I'm just glad the hardware works. If a situation goes completely sideways, I have twenty-five points on standby to dump into whatever combination keeps us alive."

"Well... that is one way to look at it," Leila writes.

"Given how long we've survived on Varathia without them, I doubt he'll ever touch the interface," Kate says. "He'll hoard those points all the way to the final round."

"Look at it positively," I tell her. "If we reach the final round with zero stats allocated, you get the satisfaction of surviving the most dangerous tournament in the world, plus the eternal right to tell me 'I told you so.'"

"Right," Kate says flatly.

}
#if(v_b3_ch1_unlock == 2 && v_b3_ch4_demolisher == 1) {
 "I'm not spending any points. I'll only start investing them when it's absolutely necessary."

"I don't know why I expected a different answer," Kate says, shaking her head.

"Barry, aren't you even a little bit curious to see what they do?" Leila's bright blue letters form in the air.

"A little," I say. "Mainly I'm just glad the hardware works. If a situation goes completely sideways, I have twenty-six points on standby to dump into whatever combination keeps us alive."

"Well... that is one way to look at it," Leila writes.

"Given how long we've survived on Varathia without them, I doubt he'll ever touch the interface," Kate says. "He'll hoard those points all the way to the final round."

"Look at it positively," I tell her. "If we reach the final round with zero stats allocated, you get the satisfaction of surviving the most dangerous tournament in the world, plus the eternal right to tell me 'I told you so.'"

"Right," Kate says flatly.

}
#if(v_b3_ch4_demolisher == 2 && v_b3_ch1_unlock != 2) {
 "Listen, the stat device was always just a backup plan. I don't want to start relying on it."

"I don't know why I expected a different answer," Kate says, shaking her head.

"Barry, aren't you even a little bit curious to see what they do?" Leila's bright blue letters form in the air.

"A little," I say. "Mainly I'm just glad the hardware works. If a situation goes completely sideways, I have twenty-five points on standby to dump into whatever combination keeps us alive."

"Well... that is one way to look at it," Leila writes.

"Given how long we've survived on Varathia without them, I doubt he'll ever touch the interface," Kate says. "He'll hoard those points all the way to the final round."

"Look at it positively," I tell her. "If we reach the final round with zero stats allocated, you get the satisfaction of surviving the most dangerous tournament in the world, plus the eternal right to tell me 'I told you so.'"

"Right," Kate says flatly.

}
#if(v_b3_ch1_unlock == 2 && v_b3_ch4_demolisher == 2) {
 "Listen, the stat device was always just a backup plan. I don't want to start relying on it."

"I don't know why I expected a different answer," Kate says, shaking her head.

"Barry, aren't you even a little bit curious to see what they do?" Leila's bright blue letters form in the air.

"A little," I say. "Mainly I'm just glad the hardware works. If a situation goes completely sideways, I have twenty-six points on standby to dump into whatever combination keeps us alive."

"Well... that is one way to look at it," Leila writes.

"Given how long we've survived on Varathia without them, I doubt he'll ever touch the interface," Kate says. "He'll hoard those points all the way to the final round."

"Look at it positively," I tell her. "If we reach the final round with zero stats allocated, you get the satisfaction of surviving the most dangerous tournament in the world, plus the eternal right to tell me 'I told you so.'"

"Right," Kate says flatly.

}
#if(v_b3_ch4_demolisher == 3) {
 "Not on this save slot. I'm using this one for the Average Joe achievement."

"What?" Kate stops dead on the gravel, staring at me as if I've slipped into an ancient dialect.

"Sorry," I say, shaking my head. "I meant I'm holding the points in reserve until we hit an absolute emergency."

"No. That wasn't what came out of your mouth," Kate says, stepping closer. "You said 'save slot' and 'Average Joe achievement.' What does that mean?"

"I was..." My tongue feels heavy. The thoughts scatter the moment I try to pin them down into a sentence. "I was just..."

Why did I say that? Was it an attempt at a joke? The memory blurs at the edges the harder I reach for it.

The rest of the group halts. Breynan glances between us, hands resting near his hilt, unsure of what has gone wrong.

"Kate, let it go," Leila writes quickly, watching me struggle. "He was making a dry joke. He wasn't serious."

"I know Barry's humor," Kate says. "That wasn't sarcasm. That wasn't even Barry's voice. It felt like someone else was speaking through his throat."

Her dark eyes lock onto mine, but she isn't looking at my face. She's staring straight through me, searching the empty air behind my skull for something invisible. A cold knot tightens in my gut.

"Easy, icey," Melindra says, resting a hand on Kate's shoulder. "I'm not picking up any compulsion magic, and a psychic intrusion would leave a ripple across his aura that we couldn't possibly miss. His joke wasn't even that obscure. When he said 'save slots', he was probably referencing those pocket beast artifacts the gnomes redesigned last year to record simulation states. And Arraka already told us the realities we walked during the Time God's ritual were alternate branches of existence. Barry was just comparing our survival to a game, treating this timeline as a run where he tries to reach the end without enhancements. Barry, isn't that what you were getting at?"

"Yes!" I seize on the explanation. "Exactly. That's what I was thinking."

Of course. Melindra's rationalization fits cleanly. It was just a joke that fell flat. Nothing more.

"I assumed it was something of the sort, though I hadn't heard of the new gnome toys," Leila writes. "I'm glad Melindra sorted it out."

"Are we finished here?" Breynan asks, looking down the corridor. "Can we keep moving?"

"I... suppose," Kate says, though her gaze lingers on me for another second.

"Good," Breynan says. "We have clear air for now, but standing in one corridor is asking for trouble. Move out."

As we fall into file, Illuna remains planted on the cavern floor. Her cold, unblinking blue eyes stay fixed on me. She doesn't turn away when my eyes meet hers; she just watches, completely motionless, tracking my steps as I brush past her. She holds that silence for three long strides before falling in behind us.

}
choice("Continue", B3-Ch04a-Disarm, v_current_scene = B3-Ch04a-Disarm)


ID: B3-Ch04a-Avg-Joe-Invested
TEXT:

 "Did the technician manage to unlock them?" Kate asks as I slide the booster back into my pocket.

"He did," I say. "Hardware is online and all three auxiliary parameters are operational."

"And are you actually going to allocate points to test them this time, or are you leaving everything unspent like before?" Her brow furrows.

"I already allocated a portion to the new parameters," I say. "With any luck, we'll get a chance to see how they perform soon."

"I'm amazed you spent anything at all," Kate says. "The way you were hoarding your pool, I expected you to walk into the finals with every single stat at zero."

"I'm not that reckless," I say. "Though I'll admit I held onto them a little longer than necessary."

"A little?" Kate raises an eyebrow.

"Fine, significantly longer than necessary. But I'm putting them to work now."

"Right," Kate says.

choice("Continue", B3-Ch04a-Disarm, v_current_scene = B3-Ch04a-Disarm)


ID: B3-Ch04a-Avg-Joe-Invested2
TEXT:

 "Did the technician manage to unlock them?" Kate asks as I slide the booster back into my pocket.

"He did," I say. "Hardware is online and all three auxiliary parameters are operational."

"And are you actually going to allocate points to test them this time, or are you leaving everything unspent like before?" Her brow furrows.

"I allocated some points," I say. "Just not into the auxiliary parameters yet."

"I'm amazed you spent anything at all," Kate says. "The way you were hoarding your pool, I expected you to walk into the finals with every single stat at zero."

"I'm not that reckless," I say. "Though I'll admit I held onto them a little longer than necessary."

"A little?" Kate raises an eyebrow.

"Barry, why didn't you put anything into the aura parameters?" Leila writes. "Aren't you even slightly curious about what they can do?"

"I don't spend points just to satisfy curiosity," I say. "I'll level them when a problem actually calls for them."

"If you say so," Leila writes.

choice("Continue", B3-Ch04a-Disarm, v_current_scene = B3-Ch04a-Disarm)


ID: B3-Ch04a-Avg-Joe-Invested3
TEXT:

 "Did the technician manage to unlock them?" Kate asks as I slide the booster back into my pocket.

"He did," I say. "Hardware is online and all three auxiliary parameters are operational."

"Did you allocate any points into them?" Kate asks.

"I did," I say. "With any luck, we'll get a chance to see how they perform soon."

"I'm sure we're all waiting with bated breath," Kate says dryly.

choice("Continue", B3-Ch04a-Disarm, v_current_scene = B3-Ch04a-Disarm)


ID: B3-Ch04a-Avg-Joe-Invested4
TEXT:

 "Did the technician manage to unlock them?" Kate asks as I slide the booster back into my pocket.

"He did," I say. "Hardware is online and all three auxiliary parameters are operational."

"Did you allocate any points into them?" Kate asks.

"No, not yet," I say.

"Why not?" Leila writes. "Aren't you even slightly curious about what they can do?"

"I don't spend points just to satisfy curiosity," I say. "I'll level them when a problem actually calls for them."

"If you say so," Leila writes.

choice("Continue", B3-Ch04a-Disarm, v_current_scene = B3-Ch04a-Disarm)


ID: B3-Ch04a-Disarm
TEXT:

 We navigate the lower corridors for another twenty minutes until every branching tunnel is walled off by warding signatures. The passage terminates at an arched doorway where an active trap hums in the stone. Breynan moves to the front, leaning in to inspect the threshold.

The construct is invisible in the gloom, but the prickle of compressed magic bites at the skin. Reading the specific payload from the ambient heat alone is impossible.

"I recognize fragments of the weave," Breynan says. "A mind-fog trigger layered over at least three earth matrices. I don't have the technical background to dismantle an array this dense. Perhaps we should scout the adjoining branches for a weaker node."

"Waste of time," Melindra says from the rear. "I routed us here because this signature is the cleanest of the lot. The other branches are nested with double the layers."

"Then we're stuck," Breynan says. "A weak array is still beyond my training. Field dispelling has never been my discipline."

#if(v_ch11_saved_rose == 0) {
 "Is Breynan really our only caster with ward training?" I ask. "Illuna, Flower said you two have cleared dozens of ruins. What did you do with the wards?"

"Ancient ruins run on mechanical counterweights and pressure plates," Illuna says, her expression flat. "Flower flips over tripwires and drops past swinging scythes. The few times we ran into active enchantment, Arraka tore it down from the amulet. Flower and I are elementalists. Complex ward dispelling is not our discipline."

"Arraka can cast from inside that gold cage?" Kate asks. "I thought the binding choked off her casting."

 "Dispelling is structure, not raw mass," Illuna says. "Her limitation in the vessel is capacity. She holds almost no reserve within the gold at any one second, so the only way she can cast anything destructive is to draw ambient energy through the metal seam for hours on end without a single lapse in focus. The knowledge never left her. That is why she can project solid-light targets, detailed illusions, and intricate dispelling arrays, yet cannot simply blast the amulet apart. A localized explosion requires three uninterrupted hours of gathering power. Disarming an elemental trap takes her thirty minutes."

"Arraka is half a city away, so you're our best option," I tell Illuna. "You've watched her dismantle wards for twenty years. You know the mechanics."

"Watching a master unravel a spell and executing the inversion yourself are not the same thing," Illuna says.

}
#if(v_ch11_saved_rose == 1) {
 "What if Illuna works with you?" I ask Breynan. "Could the two of you dismantle it together?"

"I told you already, ward theory is not my discipline," Illuna says coldly. "Combining two inexperienced casters will only double our errors."

}
choice("Continue", B3-Ch04a-Inventor, v_current_scene = B3-Ch04a-Inventor)


ID: B3-Ch04a-Inventor
TEXT:

 "Good thing we happen to be traveling with a master of counter-magic," Melindra says, clapping a heavy hand against my shoulder blade. "Isn't that right, inventor of the Brinhorn dispelling method?"

"I'm not a master," I say. "I put that technique together as a workaround years ago."

"Of course you did," Melindra says, giving both Illuna and me a firm shove toward Breynan. "Go take a look at the weave. Trying one inversion isn't going to kill you."

"Fine," I say, stepping up to the doorway with Illuna.

#if(v_ch11_saved_rose == 0) {
 Beside me, Illuna's breath catches on every short stride. Her gait drags, a subtle hitch she tries to bury beneath rigid posture. She was pretending to be recovered to keep Kate from slowing down, but up close, the strain is obvious.

"I don't know what she meant by 'Brinhorn method,'" Breynan says, pointing into the empty doorway. "Look at this oscillating current running across the seam. It looks like an electric resonance, but the frequency is completely unfamiliar."

"That is not lightning," Illuna says. "It is acid. Arraka drilled me on that specific matrix during our training."

}
#if(v_ch11_saved_rose == 1) {
 "I don't know what she meant by 'Brinhorn method,'" Breynan says, pointing into the empty doorway. "Look at this oscillating current running across the seam. It looks like an electric resonance, but the frequency is completely unfamiliar."

"That is not lightning," Illuna says. "It is acid. Arraka drilled me on that specific matrix during our training."

}
choice(""Did she also teach you how to cast it?"", B3-Ch04a-Teach, v_current_scene = B3-Ch04a-Teach)
choice(""What is the name of the spell? Do they teach it in any of the manuals?"", B3-Ch04a-Manuals, v_current_scene = B3-Ch04a-Manuals)


ID: B3-Ch04a-Teach
TEXT:

 "Did she also teach you how to cast it?"

"Yes. You cross your wrists in an X-formation, extending the fingers as the mana vents from the tips, then begin the verbal cipher," Illuna says. "Physical posture and incantations are non-essential for high-tier casters, but they anchor the flow. Arraka can manifest the matrix without twitching a muscle, but an elementalist like me requires somatic guidance to shape the path."

"Crossed wrists in an X-formation," I say. "You're describing the advanced derivative of Zaldrin's acid butterfly. The variation cataloged in the fifth edition advanced manual ten years ago."

Breynan and Illuna both freeze, staring at me.

"What?" I shrug. "I've spent ten years reading spell treatises. I know the standard somatic catalogs by heart."

"I suppose that is consistent," Illuna says. "The matrix in this ward is a direct cousin of the butterfly weave. The only structural difference is a secondary focal loop added to increase corrosive density."

 "As long as you know the standard casting somatic, you can execute the inversion," I tell them. "We just have to untangle the layer sequence."

"You speak as though this were elementary," Illuna says. "I have never produced more than three drops of acid with that cipher. It is useless in combat."

"We aren't fighting a duel," I say. "We're inverting an anchor. Keep your somatic clean and the mana reverses itself."

"Spoken like someone who has never channeled mana," Breynan scoffs. "Visualizing every distinct magical particle in reverse flow takes immense mental discipline, even on spells you cast daily."

"You don't need to visualize particles if you execute the physical somatic," I say. "Brinhorn only added that mental visualization step to his treatises because he hates moving his hands when he casts. Look. It's purely mechanical. Copy my posture."

I raise both arms, crossing my forearms at the wrists with fingers splayed wide.

 "This is ridiculous," Breynan mutters, but he steps up and mirrors the stance toward the ward.

"Keep your eyes on your hands," I instruct. "Don't overthink the mana. Channel the ambient flow into your fingertips as if you were initiating the cast, then follow your wrists as they pull back along the inverse arc. The matrix will unwind on its own. Ready?"

"If you insist," Breynan says, voice dripping with doubt.

"On three. One. Two. Three."

We pull our wrists apart in a smooth, synchronized snap, drawing our elbows back to our ribs.

Breynan freezes. His hands stay locked at his sides as the shimmering green thread in the doorway unravels into nothing.

"By the gods..." Breynan whispers, staring at his palms. "It collapsed the anchor. How did a non-mage work that out?"

"I told you," I say. "I designed the method."

"I'm starting to believe you," Breynan says, a grin breaking across his bearded face. "Everyone step back. I'll have the rest of these layers down in five minutes."

choice("Continue", B3-Ch04a-Contrary, v_current_scene = B3-Ch04a-Contrary)


ID: B3-Ch04a-Manuals
TEXT:

 "What is the name of the spell? Do they teach it in any of the manuals?"

"It is an offshoot of Zaldrin's acid butterfly," Illuna says. "Structurally identical, save for a secondary focal loop added to increase corrosive density."

"Zaldrin's butterfly," I say. "The one where you cross your wrists in an X-formation, extending the fingers as the mana vents from the tips, then begin the verbal cipher?"

Breynan and Illuna both freeze, staring at me.

"What?" I shrug. "I've spent ten years reading spell treatises. I know the standard somatic catalogs by heart."

"I suppose that is consistent," Illuna says. "Physical posture and incantations are non-essential for high-tier casters, but they anchor the flow. Arraka can manifest the matrix without twitching a muscle, but an elementalist like me requires somatic guidance to shape the path."

 "As long as you know the standard casting somatic, you can execute the inversion," I tell them. "We just have to untangle the layer sequence."

"You speak as though this were elementary," Illuna says. "I have never produced more than three drops of acid with that cipher. It is useless in combat."

