choice("Continue", B3-Ch04a-Contrary, v_current_scene = B3-Ch04a-Contrary)


ID: B3-Ch04a-Contrary
TEXT:

 Breynan's five minutes turn into nearly an hour of grueling trial and error. Once Illuna and I teach him the verbal cadence for the acid trigger, we still have to sequence the remaining elemental layers, unraveling each thread before the next anchor collapses.

When the threshold finally clears, we push down the corridor only to run straight into a far more intricate array. The weave here is packed with interlocking nodes. The single-caster method won't work—the layers are too fast and complex for Breynan to hold alone. But running the multi-step inversion with two casters introduces a lethal set of variables.

 "Listen," I tell Breynan and Illuna, "the technique is predictable when one person drives it. With two people in the loop, the risk multiplies. If your flows fall out of rhythm for even half a second, the whole array detonates. We need another way through."

"Breynan cannot hold the matrix alone and neither can I," Illuna says. "We either coordinate the casting or turn back."

"What about waking Flower?" I suggest. "The core of this construct is fire magic. That's her native element."

"Waking Flower is... ill-advised," Illuna says.

"Why? Because of your argument?"

"We are not having an argument," Illuna says stiffly.

"You are," I say. "She's calling you 'Illuna' instead of 'Petal,' and she's walking around with her chin in the air, trying to talk like Kate."

"I don't... talk like that," Kate mutters, glancing away in embarrassment.

"Regardless," I tell Illuna, "you two need to settle it. Apologize and move on."

choice("Continue", B3-Ch04a-Apologize, v_current_scene = B3-Ch04a-Apologize)


ID: B3-Ch04a-Apologize
TEXT:

 "I will not apologize," Illuna says. "She provoked the dispute."

"You told her she needs to act like an adult!"

"I did not," Illuna counters.

"You said those exact words!"

"I told her that if she conducted herself with maturity, outsiders might treat her according to her chronological age," Illuna says coldly. "I did not demand that she alter her nature. Why would she need to change? She is entirely acceptable as she is."

"So you don't actually care if she acts like a child? You're fine with her normal self?"

"Of course I am," Illuna snaps. "Why would anyone assume otherwise?"

 "Because when you lecture her on maturity, it sounds like an accusation," I say.

"Absurd," Illuna says. "I never used the word 'accusation.'"

"It isn't about the vocabulary," I say. "It's the sharp edge in your voice. Look, never mind. I'll sort it out with Flower later. Right now we need her fire casting. Can you set the feud aside for ten minutes and work with her?"

"I told you," Illuna says. "I will not wake her."

"Over a bruised ego?"

"Not over an ego," Illuna says, her tone hardening. "Flower does not cast through formal theory. She is an ethereal; her magic is purely instinctive. Your dispelling method assumes a stable, measured flow. Flower's channels fluctuate with her emotions. If we are risking detonation regardless, I would rather take my chances with Breynan."

choice("Give up and let Illuna cooperate with Breynan to disarm the trap", B3-Ch04a-Cooperate, v_current_scene = B3-Ch04a-Cooperate, v_b3_ch4_insist = 0)
choice("Keep insisting on convincing Illuna to wake Flower up", B3-Ch04a-Insist, v_current_scene = B3-Ch04a-Insist, v_b3_ch4_insist = 1)


ID: B3-Ch04a-Insist
TEXT:

#if(v_ch11_saved_rose == 0) {
 "Even with instinctive casting, one elementalist in her native element is safer than two people trying to synchronize an unfamiliar technique," I say. "I'll stand beside her and monitor the flow. If the anchor destabilizes, I'll call a halt. And you don't have to talk to her. I'll walk her through the physical movements myself."

"Truly?" Illuna raises a skeptical brow. "You will instruct an ethereal through somatic inversions without my input?"

"How hard can it be?"

"Very well," Illuna says. "I will yield the frame."

The bright blue light in her irises dims, fading to a warm, natural green.

"I'm awake, I'm awake..." Flower's voice starts as a sleepy mumble, then she staggers. Her knees buckle, and she grabs the rock wall to keep from dropping.

"Illuna, how are you hurt this badly?" Flower gasps, clutching her chest. "I can barely stay on my feet! And where are we?... This isn't the base!"

}
#if(v_ch11_saved_rose == 1) {
 "Even with instinctive casting, one elementalist in her native element is safer than two people trying to synchronize an unfamiliar technique," I say. "I'll stand beside her and monitor the flow. If the anchor destabilizes, I'll call a halt. And you don't have to talk to her. I'll walk her through the physical movements myself."

"Truly?" Illuna raises a skeptical brow. "You will instruct an ethereal through somatic inversions without my input?"

"How hard can it be?"

"Very well," Illuna says. "I will yield the frame."

The bright blue light in her irises dims, fading to a warm, natural green.

"I'm awake, I'm awake..." Flower mumbles, rubbing her eyes. "What's going on—wait, where are we? This doesn't look like the cellars at all! When did we leave the base?"

}
 "Flower, we'll explain the situation to you later," I say. "For now, we need you to help us disarm this trap. It is made only of fire spells, so you should be able to do the dispelling naturally as long as you simply copy my movements and say the correct incantations. Can you help us?"

"Uh... I think so..." Flower says, not looking very sure of herself.

"Alright," I say. "For starters, let's practice the movements. Just do what I'm doing and you'll be fine."

Flower nods anxiously and she places herself beside me, preparing to imitate my motions.

"Okay, so the first thing you're going to need to do is slightly bend your knees in order to make sure that the flow of energy remains stable," I say.

"Bend my knees," Flower repeats after me. "Got it."

When Flower does what I said, her knees are both trembling. She looks even more anxious than before.

"Is... umm... is everything alright?" I ask Flower.

"Of course!" Flower says, in a somewhat shaky voice. "Why would you need to ask? Just tell me what to do next!"

"Right..." I say. "So, what you need to do now is put your left arm in front of you with the palm facing down, while keeping your right arm alongside your body with its elbow bent."

 "Palm facing down, elbow bent, got it!" Flower says, as she follows my instructions while looking straight ahead, without so much as even a glance in my direction.

"You don't need to memorize everything I say..." I tell her. "You can just look at what I'm doing and imitate my movements."

"You're right, sorry!" Flower says, as she turns her head towards me and begins staring at my arms with an extremely focused look in her eyes. "So, what's next?"

"Uh... now you need to do a circular motion with your left arm, while you rotate your right elbow so that it points to your right side."

When Flower tries to imitate me, she somehow gets the movements completely wrong and she ends up with her left elbow bent as well.

"Flower, that's not-- my arms didn't move like that," I say.

"I'm sorry!" Flower says, in a panicky voice. "Can you do it again? I'll get it right this time!"

"I think it would be better to start off again from the beginning," I say.

"Okay..." Flower says, as she puts her arms back down, while all her body looks completely stiff.

"Right, so with both of your knees slightly bent, you first put the arm on your left side in front like this," I say.

"Got it!" Flower says, as she tries to copy my movements again.

choice("Continue", B3-Ch04a-Coach, v_current_scene = B3-Ch04a-Coach)


ID: B3-Ch04a-Coach
TEXT:

 "Flower, that's your right arm, not your left..." I say.

"Oh, you meant MY left side, not yours!" Flower says. "Sorry, I got confused there for a second."

"You do realize that we are both facing in the same direction, right?..." I say.

"Hahaha, that's right!" Flower says, while laughing very nervously. "We ARE facing in the same direction. Silly me! Hahaha!"

"Flower, you are locking your diaphragm," Illuna's voice cuts in as the bright blue flare returns to her eyes. "Four counts in, hold for two, release for four."

"Petal!" Flower's voice floods with relief. "I mean—Illuna! Yes, the breathing. Doing it now."

The blue recedes. Flower closes her eyes, drawing a long, measured breath through her nose. Her shoulders drop; the frantic tremor in her fingers settles. After two more cycles, she opens calm green eyes and snaps off a crisp salute.

"Ready for orders, captain!"

"Incredible," I mutter. "She reset in five seconds. Illuna, how did you do that?"

"Flower has always struggled with direct instruction under stress," Illuna says as the blue resurfaces for a moment. "I guide her pacing from within her thoughts. You insisted on handling the coaching alone, so I remained quiet."

 "I was really starting to panic when Illuna wouldn't say anything," Flower says. "I didn't realize that you two had a discussion about this before I woke up."

"So, Illuna's been acting as your own private coach inside your mind for the past twenty years, then?" I say.

"Well, not always, but she helps out a lot," Flower says. "And in return, I try to help her when she needs to talk to people. Like, I tell her what's okay to say and what's not, so that she doesn't upset anyone by mistake. She can be really blunt at times..."

"Wow, I did not realize that you two were so dependent on each other," I say. "Sorry about that. I guess I shouldn't have insisted on teaching Flower the spells by myself."

"Oh, right, the spell!" Flower says, and she executes the movements that she learned from me with fluid grace.

"Alright, so it was bent knees, left arm front, right elbow bent, then rotate the left arm and right elbow and... wait a second!" Flower pauses, eyes widening. "I recognize these movements! These are the same motions that I needed for an old spell of mine, except in reverse! So, if I'm not mistaken, the next moves should be something like this..."

She sweeps her hands through the exact movements needed for the spell I was about to teach her, without making a single mistake. Once she finishes, she looks to me for confirmation.
 
"Was that the spell you were trying to teach me?" Flower asks.

"That was exactly the matrix," I say.

"I haven't used physical movements to cast this spell in over ten years," Flower says. "I just shape the flow of energy with my mind nowadays. But I'm surprised that you know one of my spells, Barry! I never wrote about it anywhere."

"Flower, I already told you," Illuna says. "This spell was first invented long before you were born. You just discovered it instinctively due to your ethereal nature. Regardless, I'll take it from here. Flower should already know most of the spells required for this trap except maybe one or two. It will be faster if I just tell her which spells to use in reverse, than if you teach her the moves from scratch."

"Right, right," I say. "Go ahead, then."

With Illuna feeding the inverse sequence to Flower's mind, Flower moves through the layers with effortless rhythm. I stay near the threshold, tracking the ward's aura for erratic spikes, but Flower keeps control of her flow until the end. As the final somatic concludes, the heat radiating from the doorway dissolves into cool cavern air.

choice("Continue", B3-Ch04a-Glimpse, v_current_scene = B3-Ch04a-Glimpse)


ID: B3-Ch04a-Cooperate
TEXT:

set(v_is_dead,1) if (v_ch11_saved_rose == 0)
set(v_is_dead,0) if (v_ch11_saved_rose == 1)
#if(v_ch11_saved_rose == 0) {
 "Fine," I say. "If you really think you can handle this without Flower's help, then I won't insist anymore. But I'm warning you... one small mistake and you may well blow us all to smithereens."

"We'll be careful," Illuna says. "Breynan, let us begin by practicing our magical flow synchronization. Once we are comfortable enough with the basics, we can start practicing the actual spells."

For the next half of an hour or so, Illuna and Breynan spend their time practicing their movements and their magic flows, under my direct guidance. Knowing what works best for the multi-step dispelling technique, I was able to give them a few pointers that sped up the process, and eventually the two of them became confident enough that they could pull off the technique without making any mistakes.

That being said, the amount of concentration required for the actual dispelling of such a complex spell is much greater than what you would need when you're just practicing, so I can't say that I'm not at least a little bit worried about how well they will pull this off. What worries me the most is the fact that Illuna still doesn't seem to have recovered from her injury, even if she doesn't let it show, so I'm not sure how capable she truly is to correctly perform her part of the dispelling technique. Hopefully she'll be cautious enough to cancel her technique in time, if she thinks she won't be able to handle it.

 Once all their preparations are complete, Illuna and Breynan finally begin their synchronous dispelling technique, as the rest of us watch them with bated breath.

For the first few spells, everything goes smoothly. Both Illuna and Breynan are executing their movements perfectly, and the trap is reacting exactly how it should. After their fourth spell, however, I can already see a change in Illuna's breathing. It's fine. Everything will be fine. As long as she keeps her concentration, she should be able to--

I don't get the time to finish my train of thought, as a slight break in Illuna's concentration causes her to lose control over her magic flow, which immediately triggers the trap in front of her. None of us get the time to react and before we even know it, the explosion from the trap envelops us all, killing us in a matter of seconds.

}
#if(v_ch11_saved_rose == 1) {
 "Fine," I say. "If you really think you can handle this without Flower's help, then I won't insist anymore. But I'm warning you... one small mistake and you may well blow us all to smithereens."

"We'll be careful," Illuna says. "Breynan, let us begin by practicing our magical flow synchronization. Once we are comfortable enough with the basics, we can start practicing the actual spells."

For the next half of an hour or so, Illuna and Breynan spend their time practicing their movements and their magic flows, under my direct guidance. Knowing what works best for the multi-step dispelling technique, I was able to give them a few pointers that sped up the process, and eventually the two of them became confident enough that they could pull off the technique without making any mistakes.

That being said, the amount of concentration required for the actual dispelling of such a complex spell is much greater than what you would need when you're just practicing, so I can't say that I'm not at least a little bit worried about how well they will pull this off.

Once all their preparations are complete, Illuna and Breynan finally begin their synchronous dispelling technique, as the rest of us watch them with bated breath.

}
