choice("Load game", , , special:saves) if (v_magical_sense < 3)
choice("Continue", B3-Ch04a-Glimpse, v_current_scene = B3-Ch04a-Glimpse) if (v_magical_sense >= 3)


ID: B3-Ch04a-Glimpse
TEXT:

 "Finally," Melindra says. "Now let's pick up the pace. I have a lead on an exit to the surface."

"What makes you say that?" Kate asks as we resume our march.

"Right before we reached the fire ward, I picked up a figure stepping straight through a seredium bulkhead and vanishing," Melindra says. "The signature was heavily muffled by the stone density, but there has to be a concealed passage cut into the seam. The corridor ahead is clear of traps. We can check it directly."

"There was somebody else here in these tunnels?" Kate asks, hand falling to her hip. "Why didn't you tell us sooner?"

"Because you were in the middle of dismantling an explosive matrix," Melindra says dryly. "Dispelling requires concentration. Flagging an unconfirmed shadow when there was no immediate threat would have just gotten everyone jumpy."

"Did the figure return through the wall?" Kate asks.

"No," Melindra says. "The passage stayed cold. I haven't picked up any other movement."

choice(""What makes you think that this secret corridor will lead us to the surface?"", B3-Ch04a-Surface, v_current_scene = B3-Ch04a-Surface)
choice(""Do you think it's possible that this person detected us and went to get reinforcements?"", B3-Ch04a-Reinforcements, v_current_scene = B3-Ch04a-Reinforcements)
choice(""Was this person a mage? What do you think they were doing down here?"", B3-Ch04a-Mage, v_current_scene = B3-Ch04a-Mage)


ID: B3-Ch04a-Surface
TEXT:

 "What makes you think that this secret corridor will lead us to the surface?"

"There's no guarantee it opens onto the street," Melindra says. "A concealed stairwell is still better than wandering blind through dead-end tunnels."

"Fair enough," I say.

"You said the signature was muffled..." Leila's glowing blue letters form between us. "Could it belong to someone we've already crossed? One of the royal guard commanders?"

"The resonance felt familiar," Melindra admits, tapping her chin. "It could have been a guard officer, but reading through seredium is imprecise."

"If Gontrok or the guard captain is on the other side of that seam, we're walking into a slaughter," Breynan mutters.

"Only if they're waiting in ambush," Melindra says. "A fighter of that caliber doesn't bother hiding if he knows where we are."

"Point taken," Breynan concedes.

"Step it up, then," Melindra says. "The base will start dispatching search parties if we stay missing much longer."

We trail Melindra for ten minutes through the winding dark until she pulls up before a seamless slab of black rock. She runs her fingers across the damp stone, searching for the seam.

"Clear the way," Illuna says, stepping past us. "I watched Fyron trigger seredium bypasses dozens of times. You're searching blind."

She steps up to the slab, channels a precise pulse of mana into the stone, and delivers two measured raps against the center. The seredium grinds back an inch, then glides smoothly into the recessed wall, revealing a stone stairwell cutting upward through the rock.

"Not bad," Melindra smirks. "Let's see where it goes."

choice("Continue", B3-Ch04a-Crystals, v_current_scene = B3-Ch04a-Crystals)


ID: B3-Ch04a-Reinforcements
TEXT:

 "Do you think it's possible that this person detected us and went to get reinforcements?"

"Highly doubtful," Melindra says. "If I could barely register his presence through three layers of seredium, he wasn't picking us up from that distance."

"You said the signature was muffled..." Leila's glowing blue letters form between us. "Could it belong to someone we've already crossed? One of the royal guard commanders?"

"The resonance felt familiar," Melindra admits, tapping her chin. "It could have been a guard officer, but reading through seredium is imprecise."

"If Gontrok or the guard captain is on the other side of that seam, we're walking into a slaughter," Breynan mutters.

"Only if they're waiting in ambush," Melindra says. "A fighter of that caliber doesn't bother hiding if he knows where we are."

"Point taken," Breynan concedes.

"Step it up, then," Melindra says. "The base will start dispatching search parties if we stay missing much longer."

We trail Melindra for ten minutes through the winding dark until she pulls up before a seamless slab of black rock. She runs her fingers across the damp stone, searching for the seam.

"Clear the way," Illuna says, stepping past us. "I watched Fyron trigger seredium bypasses dozens of times. You're searching blind."

She steps up to the slab, channels a precise pulse of mana into the stone, and delivers two measured raps against the center. The seredium grinds back an inch, then glides smoothly into the recessed wall, revealing a stone stairwell cutting upward through the rock.

"Not bad," Melindra smirks. "Let's see where it goes."

choice("Continue", B3-Ch04a-Crystals, v_current_scene = B3-Ch04a-Crystals)


ID: B3-Ch04a-Mage
TEXT:

 "Was this person a mage? What do you think they were doing down here?"

"He was a caster," Melindra says. "Probably running a routine inspection on the ward grid before heading off shift. We won't know until we reach the other side."

"You said the signature was muffled..." Leila's glowing blue letters form between us. "Could it belong to someone we've already crossed? One of the royal guard commanders?"

"The resonance felt familiar," Melindra admits, tapping her chin. "It could have been a guard officer, but reading through seredium is imprecise."

"If Gontrok or the guard captain is on the other side of that seam, we're walking into a slaughter," Breynan mutters.

"Only if they're waiting in ambush," Melindra says. "A fighter of that caliber doesn't bother hiding if he knows where we are."

"Point taken," Breynan concedes.

"Step it up, then," Melindra says. "The base will start dispatching search parties if we stay missing much longer."

We trail Melindra for ten minutes through the winding dark until she pulls up before a seamless slab of black rock. She runs her fingers across the damp stone, searching for the seam.

"Clear the way," Illuna says, stepping past us. "I watched Fyron trigger seredium bypasses dozens of times. You're searching blind."

She steps up to the slab, channels a precise pulse of mana into the stone, and delivers two measured raps against the center. The seredium grinds back an inch, then glides smoothly into the recessed wall, revealing a stone stairwell cutting upward through the rock.

"Not bad," Melindra smirks. "Let's see where it goes."

choice("Continue", B3-Ch04a-Crystals, v_current_scene = B3-Ch04a-Crystals)


ID: B3-Ch04a-Crystals
TEXT:

#if(v_b3_ch4_insist == 0) {
 "Should we wake Flower before we ascend?" Kate asks. "If there's a garrison at the top, we need every active front we have."

"Perhaps," Illuna concedes. "I will yield."

The blue light fades from her eyes, replaced by Flower's clear green gaze.

"I'm awake, I'm awake..." Flower rubs her eyelids, blinking at the dark stone. "What's going on—where are we? This isn't the base!"

"Quick version," I whisper as we step onto the first riser. "We took a bad teleport into an uncharted tunnel network. This stairwell might lead to street level, but we could walk straight into a patrol at the top. Stay sharp."

"Got it," Flower says, nodding firmly. "I'm ready."

Illuna touches the outer seam to slide the seredium slab back into place, sealing us in the stairwell. We climb in single file, our boots muffled on the stone steps.

Luminescent blue crystals jut from the mortar at regular intervals, casting cold azure light across the risers—the exact same crystalline nodes embedded throughout the rebel tunnels and the subterranean vaults below Thilias.

The deep earth of Varathia is practically saturated with seredium and glowing mineral deposits. It can't be a coincidence that this continent hosts talking beasts, primordial entities, and the only known terrestrial interface to the Magium. I'll have to press Arraka on the connection once we regroup with Daren.

We keep our footsteps silent on the climb. Melindra warns us that the upper threshold is reinforced with heavy seredium plating, blinding both our senses and whatever sits on the other side.

At the top landing, Illuna signals a halt. She delivers two light knocks against the stone seam.

The panel slides aside with a low hiss, opening into a high-ceilinged stone chamber. Not open street level, but a subterranean maintenance closet packed with wash buckets, mops, and damp scrubbing rags.

}
#if(v_b3_ch4_insist == 1) {
 Illuna touches the outer seam to slide the seredium slab back into place, sealing us in the stairwell. We climb in single file, our boots muffled on the stone steps.

Luminescent blue crystals jut from the mortar at regular intervals, casting cold azure light across the risers—the exact same crystalline nodes embedded throughout the rebel tunnels and the subterranean vaults below Thilias.

The deep earth of Varathia is practically saturated with seredium and glowing mineral deposits. It can't be a coincidence that this continent hosts talking beasts, primordial entities, and the only known terrestrial interface to the Magium. I'll have to press Arraka on the connection once we regroup with Daren.

We keep our footsteps silent on the climb. Melindra warns us that the upper threshold is reinforced with heavy seredium plating, blinding both our senses and whatever sits on the other side.

At the top landing, Illuna signals a halt. She delivers two light knocks against the stone seam.

The panel slides aside with a low hiss, opening into a high-ceilinged stone chamber. Not open street level, but a subterranean maintenance closet packed with wash buckets, mops, and damp scrubbing rags.

}
 Illuna closes the passage behind us and advances toward the heavy timber door at the far end of the closet. Beyond the wood, dozens of faint magical signatures hum in the air.

"The adjoining hall is packed with mages," Melindra whispers, leaning close. "If they move on us the second we step through, Kate drops an ice wall and we fall back to the stairs. By the time they figure out the latch, we'll be—"

A sharp gasp cuts her off.

Flower has pushed the door ajar, her hand flying to her mouth.

"What is it?" Kate steps up, frost gathering on her fingertips. "Guards?"

"No..." Flower whispers, her small frame shaking violently. "It's... it's..."

We push past her into the doorway, and the breath catches in my throat.

choice("Continue", B3-Ch04a-Machine, v_current_scene = B3-Ch04a-Machine)


ID: B3-Ch04a-Machine
TEXT:

 The chamber opens into a vast subterranean vault.

Hundreds of men and young boys hang from the ceiling in tattered rags, suspended by iron links clamped to their wrists. Their boots dangle inches above the blood-stained flags. Skin stretched bone-tight, gray as ash, eyes staring vacant into the gloom—they look more like preserved corpses than living captives.

As we step onto the walkway, the overhead grid catches the crystal light. The ceiling is a continuous sheet of dull gray alloy, ribbed with conduits that converge on a massive spherical receptacle in the center of the hall. The vessel throbs with a heavy, rhythmic pulse of extracted mana. Bile rises in my throat.

The chains aren't just bindings. Magical current flows through the links in visible, shimmering waves, siphoning every drop of ambient mana from their bodies and funneling it into the central accumulator. An industrial refinery built to bleed human beings dry until they lack the breath to speak.

None of the suspended men react to our boots on the stone. Most hang motionless, lost to exhaustion. Every few moments, a sudden convulsion tears through one of the captives; he screams into the damp air for five agonizing seconds, then slumps back into the stupor as the current drains him.

 Towards the center of the room there are some tables made out of metal on which a few other people are lying on their backs, who seem somewhat more alive than the ones hanging from the ceiling, although not by much. These men also have chains tied to their wrists, but in their case the chains are linked directly to the metal container, not to the ceiling. As we're approaching them, Melindra stops to look at one of the chains that are holding the prisoners suspended from the ceiling.

"These chains..." Melindra says. "Something about them feels... familiar..."

While Melindra is still looking at the chains, one of the prisoners on the metal tables calls out to us in a feeble voice.

"Who are you?..." the man says. "I've never seen you here before. Are you the new cleaners?"

"No," I say, as I get closer to him. "We don't work here. We stumbled into this place by accident."

"You're... you're not with them?..." the man says. "Please, you have to help me! These people who trapped us here... they are using us like cattle. Milking us dry of our energy to feed this machine.  My boy and I, we did nothing wrong! We were just taken from our homes one night without being given any reason. I don't even know where they took him. And I don't know if anything happened to my wife and daughter after that. Please, I need to know if they are safe... I need to get out of here!"

#if(v_b3_ch3_chains == 0) {
 "Did... this happen recently?" I ask him. "Is your daughter's name 'Alice', by any chance?"

"Yes!" the man shouts, somewhat excitedly. "That's her name! You've met her? Is she safe?"

"She is safe, for now," I say. "She's been looking for you."

"You have to take me to her!" the man says. "Please, you need to cut off these chains and set me free! I beg you!"

Kate, Leila and I exchange a look and then I walk forward, in order to get closer to the chains that are tied to the man's wrists. The chains don't look very strong. I'm pretty confident that my enchanted dagger should be able to slice through these as through butter.

I take my dagger out of its sheath and I hold it in my hand for a few seconds, considering my options, while the scream of a young boy echoes faintly in the distance.

}
#if(v_b3_ch3_chains == 1) {
 "Did... this happen recently?" I ask him. "Is your daughter's name 'Alice', by any chance?"

"Yes!" the man shouts, somewhat excitedly. "That's her name! You've met her? Is she safe?"

"She is safe, for now," I say. "She's been looking for you."

"You have to take me to her!" the man says. "Please, you need to cut off these chains and set me free! I beg you!"

Kate, Leila and I exchange a look and then I walk forward, in order to get closer to the chains that are tied to the man's wrists. The chains don't look very strong. I'm pretty confident that my enchanted dagger should be able to slice through these as through butter.

I take my dagger out of its sheath and I hold it in my hand for a few seconds, considering my options, while the scream of a young boy echoes faintly in the distance.

That scream... I've heard that scream before. And the rattling sound of the chains as well. I've heard them both in that premonition! The one I saw right before giving Leila's father my stat device. But if this is true, then the next thing that should happen is--

"Barry, wait! Don't do it!" I suddenly hear Melindra, shouting loudly in a panicked tone.

}
choice("Cut the chains with my dagger and free Alice's father.", B3-Ch04a-Container, v_current_scene = B3-Ch04a-Container, v_b3_ch4_cut = 1) if (v_b3_ch3_chains == 0)
choice("Don't cut the chains and leave Alice's father on the metal table.", B3-Ch04a-Container, v_current_scene = B3-Ch04a-Container, v_b3_ch4_cut = 0) if (v_b3_ch3_chains == 0)
choice("Cut the chains with my dagger.", B3-Ch04a-Container, v_current_scene = B3-Ch04a-Container, v_b3_ch4_cut = 1) if (v_b3_ch3_chains == 1)
choice("Ask Melindra what's wrong.", B3-Ch04a-Container, v_current_scene = B3-Ch04a-Container, v_b3_ch4_cut = 0) if (v_b3_ch3_chains == 1)


ID: B3-Ch04a-Container
TEXT:

#if(v_b3_ch3_chains == 0 && v_b3_ch4_cut == 1) {
 "Barry, wait! Don't do it!" Melindra's voice screams out in panic just as my blade shears through the iron links.

The warning arrives too late. The enchanted edge bites through the metal without resistance.

The instant the severed links drop, Alice's father arches violently off the table. A savage convulsion seizes his limbs, his spine bowing as his teeth snap shut. Five seconds of horrific thrashing, and then he collapses limp.

His chest goes still. Under my magical sense, the faint spark of his aura snuffs out like a blown candle.

I stand frozen above the table, my hand locked around the dagger hilt.

"What... what happened?" The words barely form in my throat. "Why did he stop?"

"The alloy," Melindra says quietly, stepping to the table and lifting the severed chain. "It's a parasitic composite. If it remains bound to a living aura past the calibration window, it establishes a symbiotic graft. Forcing the connection apart tears the nervous system out with it. I should have recognized the resonance sooner."

}
#if(v_b3_ch3_chains == 1 && v_b3_ch4_cut == 1) {
 I drive the blade downward, severing the links before Melindra can move.

A tactical reflex—if I hesitate, she'll drop an air barrier to stop me from taking on a liability during an escape. That was the choice the premonition showed me.

The calculated move proves fatal.

The instant the severed links drop, Alice's father arches violently off the table. A savage convulsion seizes his limbs, his spine bowing as his teeth snap shut. Five seconds of horrific thrashing, and then he collapses limp.

His chest goes still. Under my magical sense, the faint spark of his aura snuffs out like a blown candle.

I stand frozen above the table, my hand locked around the dagger hilt.

"What... what happened?" The words barely form in my throat. "Why did he stop?"

"The alloy," Melindra says quietly, stepping to the table and lifting the severed chain. "It's a parasitic composite. If it remains bound to a living aura past the calibration window, it establishes a symbiotic graft. Forcing the connection apart tears the nervous system out with it. I should have recognized the resonance sooner."

}
#if(v_b3_ch3_chains == 0 && v_b3_ch4_cut == 0) {
 "Barry, wait! Don't do it!" Melindra's voice rings across the room just as I pull the dagger back from the iron.

"I'm holding," I tell her, stepping back. "What did you spot?"

"The metal," Melindra says, picking up a dangling link. "It's an evolved iteration of the prototypes Billy Bob was tinkering with when I was exiled. The baseline mechanics haven't changed. Once this alloy bonds to an aura past the calibration threshold, it becomes a biological graft. Cutting the link mechanically is equivalent to ripping out an organ. The shock kills the subject within seconds."

"Are you serious?" I stare at the iron cuffs. "Then how do we free them?"

"Manual deactivation from the master terminal in the control room," Melindra says. "There is no other bypass."

}
#if(v_b3_ch3_chains == 1 && v_b3_ch4_cut == 0) {
 "Why?" I ask, holding the blade still. "What is it?"

"The metal," Melindra says, picking up a dangling link. "It's an evolved iteration of the prototypes Billy Bob was tinkering with when I was exiled. The baseline mechanics haven't changed. Once this alloy bonds to an aura past the calibration threshold, it becomes a biological graft. Cutting the link mechanically is equivalent to ripping out an organ. The shock kills the subject within seconds."

"Are you serious?" I stare at the iron cuffs. "Then how do we free them?"

"Manual deactivation from the master terminal in the control room," Melindra says. "There is no other bypass."

}
#if(v_b3_ch4_cut == 1) {
 "There has to be a counter-weave," I say desperately, grabbing the severed links. "A mending matrix, an aura bridge—something to restore the current!"

"The conduit is severed," Melindra says softly. "The metal is a modified derivative of Billy Bob's old research. Once the biological bridge collapses, the trauma is permanent. The only way to release these prisoners alive is from the control terminal."

"Can we shatter the central accumulator?" Leila's bright blue letters flash in the air. "Would destroying the tank sever the extraction grid?"

"Breaching an accumulator holding that much raw mana will level the entire foundation," Melindra says, dropping the chain. "Every soul in this vault dies in the blast. The terminal is the only route."

"Then we move on the control room before the garrison—" Kate starts, but Melindra cuts her off.

"We don't have the layout for this facility," Melindra snaps. "A primary terminal will be ringed with elite wards and anti-magic troops. We're drained from the surface fight, we're missing half our fighters, and we have zero rebel backing. Playing savior right now is suicide."

"No..." I drop to my knees, frantically pressing the severed iron edges together against the dead man's wrist. "There has to be a pulse. I was trying to free him. He was supposed to go home to his daughter... How do I look that girl in the face and tell her I killed her father? There has to be—"

A blinding surge of azure light floods the vault.

The accumulator core roars to life, sending a searing shockwave through every conduit in the ceiling.

Hundreds of voices tear into a simultaneous, agonized scream.

}
#if(v_b3_ch4_cut == 0) {
 "Can we shatter the central accumulator?" Leila's bright blue letters flash in the air. "Would destroying the tank sever the extraction grid?"

"Breaching an accumulator holding that much raw mana will level the entire foundation," Melindra says, dropping the chain. "Every soul in this vault dies in the blast. The terminal is the only route."

"Then we move on the control room before the garrison—" Kate starts, but Melindra cuts her off.

"We don't have the layout for this facility," Melindra snaps. "A primary terminal will be ringed with elite wards and anti-magic troops. We're drained from the surface fight, we're missing half our fighters, and we have zero rebel backing. Playing savior right now is suicide."

"You're leaving me?" Alice's father thrashes against the iron cuffs, terror cracking his voice. "Don't walk away! Cut the links! She could be wrong—you don't know I'll die! Cut them! Cut them before the core charges again—no, it's starting! Cut them quick, before—"

A blinding surge of azure light floods the vault.

The accumulator core roars to life, sending a searing shockwave through every conduit in the ceiling.

Hundreds of voices tear into a simultaneous, agonized scream.

}
choice("Continue", B3-Ch04a-Spectacle, v_current_scene = B3-Ch04a-Spectacle, v_b3_ch4_beg = 0, v_b3_ch4_billy = 0, v_b3_ch4_tasked = 0, v_b3_ch4_drones = 0)


ID: B3-Ch04a-Spectacle
TEXT:

 "Mercy!" a hanging captive shrieks, thrashing in his chains.

"Please, by the gods, mercy!" another screams from the far gallery.

"Make it stop! Have mercy!"

The chorus swells into a deafening, unified plea that echoes off the metal ceiling, raw and futile. There is no one in the chamber to hear them but us.

For thirty brutal seconds, the blue conduits burn white-hot. Then the flare dims, the hum decays, and the hall sinks back into heavy, suffocating silence.

Beside me, Flower trembles so hard her teeth click. Kate's knuckles are white, her jaw clenched tight enough to crack. Breynan stares at the floor, fighting down bile, while Leila stands frozen, her eyes wide with the distant horror of Beacon memories.

Just as the sound dies, a cold voice cuts through the stillness behind us.

"Did you enjoy the spectacle, Barry?" Eiden says, with a grave tone in his voice.

I turn around quickly, but I don't see him anywhere. Of course he's hiding invisibly... Without Arraka we never had a chance to detect him, and he knows this. Has he just been standing there from the beginning?...

"What's the matter?" Eiden says, with the same serious tone in his voice as before. "Didn't you say that you were going to save every person in Varathia despite your lack of powers? Well, I've given you the opportunity. What are you waiting for?"

Melindra gasps, whirling toward the empty space between two hanging lines.

"It was you!" Melindra says loudly, while she points at Eiden. "You're the one who led us here from the underground tunnels! That's why your aura seemed so familiar. You were just masking it so I couldn't recognize you from afar!"

"What will happen first, I wonder?" Eiden continues to address me, while ignoring Melindra. "Will your resolve eventually break when you'll finally understand just what exactly you've gotten yourself into? Or will you be able to achieve the impossible, like you've claimed? Regardless of what happens, I hope you will continue to keep me entertained. Do not disappoint me."

"I can't believe it!" Melindra says. "He's ignoring me again! Well, this time he won't get away with it!"

She hurls a condensed blast of compressed air right next to where Eiden was standing invisibly, but all this manages to accomplish is to slightly rock back and forth a few of the prisoners that were hanging from the ceiling in the vicinity, without having any visible effects on Eiden himself.

"Damn it!" Melindra says. "He's already gone!"

"Yeah, he probably just teleported here to give his speech, and now he went back to watching us from afar," I say. "I swear I'm going to pay him back for this once I figure out all those magic loopholes of his..."

"Forget about Eiden," Kate says. "We still don't know which way we need to go to find the exit. Or the control room..."

choice(""I say we should focus on finding the exit, for the time being."", B3-Ch04a-Attention, v_current_scene = B3-Ch04a-Attention, v_b3_ch4_control = 0)
choice(""We have to find the control room, first. We can't just leave these people here like this."", B3-Ch04a-Attention, v_current_scene = B3-Ch04a-Attention, v_b3_ch4_control = 1)


ID: B3-Ch04a-Attention
TEXT:

#if(v_b3_ch4_control == 0) {
 "I say we should focus on finding the exit, for the time being."

"As much as I want these men freed, Barry is right," Breynan says grimly. "We don't have the numbers or the conditioning for a mass extraction—"

"Attention! Attention, lower facility livestock!"

A grating, nasal voice blares from a brass acoustic horn mounted high on the central pillar.

"The wash crews arrive in one hour. Prepare yourselves. If there is a single disciplinary infraction, today's nutrient broth is canceled. That means no thrashing while the scrubbers are cleaning, no kicking the attendants, and absolutely no biting! Senior cleaner Marlo is still in bandages from the last—"

"Master Biliborrathrathsaran, urgent pardon for the intrusion, but—" a servant's voice bleeds into the horn's pickup.

"The name is Biliborrathrathsarran, you absolute dullard!" Billy Bob screeches. "You omitted the secondary trill on the final consonant! How difficult is it to pronounce basic lessathi phonetics?"

"Sir, please," the servant stammers. "The king demands your presence in the throne room immediately—"

"Let him wait," Billy Bob snaps. "I am calibrating an essential compression sequence. I will attend to royalty when the run concludes."

"Could you not dispatch one of your droons to deliver the delay?" the servant pleads. "There is surely a unit in the corridor—"

"For the thousandth time, they are called DRONES!" Billy Bob screams. "Drones! Not droons! Are the vowels too complex for your peasant skull?!"

"Oh," Melindra murmurs. "So that's the word for them."

"Quiet," I hiss. "Listen."

"Fine! I will dispatch an escort," Billy Bob sighs loudly. "Unit three hundred six, remove this nuisance from my laboratory. Relay a transmission to the nearest perimeter drone and inform the king I will arrive in two hours. Now where was I? Right, the disciplinary broadcast. Did I finish the mandate?... Whatever. I have real work to do."

A sharp metallic click echoes from the horn, and the line goes dead.

"Right," I say, exhaling slowly. "Billy Bob opened that broadcast by addressing the 'lower facility below the tower.' That means we're sitting beneath one of the four black seredium monoliths on the city perimeter."

"Almost certainly," Breynan says. "Which clarifies why Billy Bob has been harvesting mages for the past three years. This central accumulator feeds the power grid for the city's drones, heavy transports, siege golems, and defense relics. It also means the main ground-level gate will be barricaded by a garrison in anti-magic plating. If we want to extract, we have to fight our way to an upper tier where the seredium thins out enough to establish a transceiver link with Barry in the relay chamber."

"How do we ascend through a fortified monolith without tripping every alarm on the manifest?" I ask.

}
#if(v_b3_ch4_control == 1) {
 "We have to find the control room, first. We can't just leave these people here like this."

"I agree," Kate says. "Stealth is mandatory. We avoid open engagements unless our position is fully compromised, or else—"

"Attention! Attention, lower facility livestock!"

A grating, nasal voice blares from a brass acoustic horn mounted high on the central pillar.

"The wash crews arrive in one hour. Prepare yourselves. If there is a single disciplinary infraction, today's nutrient broth is canceled. That means no thrashing while the scrubbers are cleaning, no kicking the attendants, and absolutely no biting! Senior cleaner Marlo is still in bandages from the last—"

"Master Biliborrathrathsaran, urgent pardon for the intrusion, but—" a servant's voice bleeds into the horn's pickup.

"The name is Biliborrathrathsarran, you absolute dullard!" Billy Bob screeches. "You omitted the secondary trill on the final consonant! How difficult is it to pronounce basic lessathi phonetics?"

"Sir, please," the servant stammers. "The king demands your presence in the throne room immediately—"

"Let him wait," Billy Bob snaps. "I am calibrating an essential compression sequence. I will attend to royalty when the run concludes."

"Could you not dispatch one of your droons to deliver the delay?" the servant pleads. "There is surely a unit in the corridor—"

"For the thousandth time, they are called DRONES!" Billy Bob screams. "Drones, not droons! Are the vowels too complex for your peasant skull?!"

"Oh," Melindra murmurs. "So that's the word for them."

"Quiet," I hiss. "Listen."

"Fine! I will dispatch an escort," Billy Bob sighs loudly. "Unit three hundred six, remove this nuisance from my laboratory. Relay a transmission to the nearest perimeter drone and inform the king I will arrive in two hours. Now where was I? Right, the disciplinary broadcast. Did I finish the mandate?... Whatever. I have real work to do."

A sharp metallic click echoes from the horn, and the line goes dead.

"Right," I say, exhaling slowly. "Billy Bob opened that broadcast by addressing the 'lower facility below the tower.' That means we're sitting beneath one of the four black seredium monoliths on the city perimeter."

"Almost certainly," Breynan says. "Which clarifies why Billy Bob has been harvesting mages for the past three years. This central accumulator feeds the power grid for the city's drones, heavy transports, siege golems, and defense relics. It also means the main ground-level gate will be barricaded by a garrison in anti-magic plating. If we want to extract, we have to fight our way to an upper tier where the seredium thins out enough to establish a transceiver link with Barry in the relay chamber."

"I thought we agreed the master terminal came first," Kate says.

"You and Barry agreed," Melindra shoots back. "Have either of you considered the logistics of moving several hundred emaciated captives past a perimeter army? Half of these people can barely draw breath, let alone run."

}
choice("Continue", B3-Ch04b-Assistance, v_current_scene = B3-Ch04b-Assistance)
